      <p>
        Statistical genetics formulates phenotypes as probabilistic mathematical functions of discrete genotypes, unobserved environmental exposures, evolutionary dynamics, and ancestry structures:
        <br/>
        &bull; <strong>Population Foundations:</strong> Hardy-Weinberg Equilibrium (<Katex tex="p^2 + 2pq + q^2 = 1" />), Wright-Fisher genetic drift (<Katex tex="H_t = H_0(1 - 1/(2N_e))^t" />), and Kingman's coalescent genealogy (<Katex tex="\mathbb{E}[T_{\text{MRCA}}] = 4N_e(1 - 1/n)" />) govern allele frequency dynamics across generations.
        <br/>
        &bull; <strong>Variance Decomposition:</strong> Total phenotypic variance partitions into genetic and environmental components (<Katex tex="V_P = V_G + V_E + 2\operatorname{Cov}(G, E)" />), where <Katex tex="V_G" /> decomposes into additive (<Katex tex="V_A" />), dominance (<Katex tex="V_D" />), and epistatic (<Katex tex="V_I" />) variance under orthogonal parameterizations.
        <br/>
        &bull; <strong>Linkage Disequilibrium:</strong> Non-random gametic association (<Katex tex="D, D', r^2" />) decays exponentially under meiotic recombination (<Katex tex="D_t = D_0(1 - \theta)^t" />), structured by PRDM9-directed double-strand break hotspots into discrete haplotype blocks.
        <br/>
        &bull; <strong>Heritability & Mixed Models:</strong> Genomic Relatedness Matrices (<Katex tex="\mathbf{A} = \mathbf{W}\mathbf{W}^T/M" />) and Linear Mixed Models (<Katex tex="\mathbf{y} = \mathbf{X}\boldsymbol{\beta} + \mathbf{g} + \boldsymbol{\epsilon}" />) eliminate population stratification while capturing polygenic architecture.
        <br/>
        &bull; <strong>Causality & Prediction:</strong> Instrumental variable techniques (Mendelian Randomization) evaluate causal hypotheses under structural assumptions, while Bayesian shrinkage (PRS-CS) and regulatory sequence models (Borzoi, AlphaGenome) bridge statistical associations to predictive biology.
      </p>
