    <!-- Chapter 1 -->
    <section id="ch1">
      <h2>1. Foundations of Population Genetics & The Infinitesimal Model</h2>

      <h3>Hardy-Weinberg Equilibrium (HWE)</h3>
      <p>
        In an infinitely large, panmictic (random-mating) diploid population with non-overlapping generations and in the absence of mutation, migration, and natural selection, allele and genotype frequencies remain constant over generations.
      </p>
      <p>
        For a biallelic locus with alleles <Katex tex="A" /> and <Katex tex="a" /> having frequencies <Katex tex="p = P(A)" /> and <Katex tex="q = P(a) = 1 - p" />, random fusion of independent gametes yields the exact diploid genotype probabilities:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Hardy-Weinberg Genotype Proportions</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="P(AA) = p^2, \quad P(Aa) = 2pq, \quad P(aa) = q^2 \implies p^2 + 2pq + q^2 = (p + q)^2 = 1" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          Degrees of Freedom: <Katex tex="\text{df} = 3 - 1 - 1 = 1" /> (3 genotype classes minus 1 for fixed total sample size minus 1 for estimated allele frequency <Katex tex="p" />).
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        In GWAS quality control, deviations from HWE are tested using Pearson's <Katex tex="\chi^2" /> goodness-of-fit test or Fisher's exact test. Significant HWE departures (<Katex tex="P < 10^{-6}" /> in controls) typically indicate <strong>genotyping errors</strong> (such as batch-specific probe hybridization failures or allele dropout), strong natural selection, or severe population stratification.
      </p>

      <h3>Wright-Fisher Model & Genetic Drift</h3>
      <p>
        In finite populations of size <Katex tex="N" /> (containing <Katex tex="2N" /> gametes), sampling variance causes allele frequencies to fluctuate randomly across generations—a stochastic process known as <strong>genetic drift</strong>.
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Wright-Fisher Transition Probability & Heterozygosity Loss</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="P(k \mid j) = \binom{2N}{k} \left(\frac{j}{2N}\right)^k \left(1 - \frac{j}{2N}\right)^{2N - k}, \quad H_t = H_0 \left(1 - \frac{1}{2N_e}\right)^t \approx H_0 e^{-t / (2N_e)}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="P(k \mid j)" /> = Probability that an allele with <Katex tex="j" /> copies in generation <Katex tex="t" /> has <Katex tex="k" /> copies in generation <Katex tex="t+1" /> &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="H_t = 2p_t q_t" /> = Expected heterozygosity at generation <Katex tex="t" /> &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="N_e" /> = Effective population size.
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        Under neutral genetic drift, the probability that a neutral allele eventually reaches fixation equals its current frequency: <Katex tex="P(\text{fixation}) = p_0" />. For a new neutral mutation arising in a single copy (<Katex tex="p_0 = 1/(2N)" />), the average time to fixation conditional on fixing is <Katex tex="4N_e" /> generations.
      </p>

      <h3>Coalescent Theory & Tajima's D</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Kingman's Coalescent (1982)</strong> models the genealogy of <Katex tex="k" /> sampled lineages looking backward in time. The time <Katex tex="T_k" /> during which there are <Katex tex="k" /> distinct ancestors is exponentially distributed with rate <Katex tex="\lambda_k = \binom{k}{2} / (2N_e)" />:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Expected Time to Most Recent Common Ancestor (TMRCA) & Total Tree Length</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\mathbb{E}[T_{\text{MRCA}}] = \sum_{k=2}^{n} \frac{4N_e}{k(k-1)} = 4N_e \left(1 - \frac{1}{n}\right), \qquad \mathbb{E}[L_{\text{tot}}] = \sum_{k=2}^{n} k \mathbb{E}[T_k] = 4N_e \sum_{i=1}^{n-1} \frac{1}{i}" display={true} />
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        <strong>Tajima's D statistic (1989)</strong> compares two estimators of the population mutation parameter <Katex tex="\theta = 4N_e \mu" />: the average pairwise nucleotide diversity (<Katex tex="\hat{\theta}_\pi" />) and Watterson's estimator based on the number of segregating sites (<Katex tex="\hat{\theta}_S = S / a_1" />):
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Tajima's D Neutrality Test</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="D = \frac{\hat{\theta}_\pi - \hat{\theta}_S}{\sqrt{\widehat{\operatorname{Var}}(\hat{\theta}_\pi - \hat{\theta}_S)}}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="D \approx 0" />: Neutral evolution &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="D < 0" />: Excess of rare alleles (selective sweep or population expansion) &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="D > 0" />: Excess of intermediate-frequency alleles (balancing selection or population bottleneck).
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>The Fisher 1918 Synthesis & The Infinitesimal Model</h3>
      <p>
        In a landmark paper, <strong>Ronald A. Fisher (1918)</strong> reconciled Mendelian particulate genetics with Galtonian continuous biometrical traits. Fisher demonstrated that if a trait is governed by an infinite number of Mendelian loci (<Katex tex="M \to \infty" />), each with an infinitesimally small additive effect <Katex tex="\beta_j \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2/M)" />, the sum of these independent alleles converges—by the <strong>Central Limit Theorem</strong>—to a continuous Gaussian phenotypic distribution:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Central Limit Convergence of Polygenic Traits</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="y_i = \mu + \sum_{j=1}^{M} \beta_j X_{ij} + \epsilon_i \xrightarrow{M \to \infty} y_i \sim \mathcal{N}(\mu, V_P)" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="X_{ij} \in \{0, 1, 2\}" /> = Genotype dosage at SNP <Katex tex="j" /> &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="\beta_j" /> = Additive allelic effect size &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="\epsilon_i \sim \mathcal{N}(0, V_E)" /> = Environmental noise.
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Complete Phenotypic Variance Partitioning</h3>
      <p>
        Starting from the fundamental decomposition of individual phenotype <Katex tex="P = G + E" />, the total phenotypic variance <Katex tex="V_P = \operatorname{Var}(P)" /> expands as:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Master Variance Decomposition</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="V_P = V_G + V_E + 2\operatorname{Cov}(G, E) = V_A + V_D + V_I + V_{G \times E} + 2\operatorname{Cov}(G, E) + V_E" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="V_A" /> = Additive genetic variance (variance of breeding values) &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="V_D" /> = Dominance variance (intra-locus interactions under HWE) &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="V_I = V_{AA} + V_{AD} + V_{DD}" /> = Epistatic variance &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="V_{G \times E}" /> = Gene-by-environment interaction variance &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="\operatorname{Cov}(G, E)" /> = Genotype-environment covariance (e.g. passive/active genetic nurture).
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Narrow-Sense vs. Broad-Sense Heritability & The Breeder's Equation</h3>
      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Heritability Formulations & The Breeder's Equation</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="h^2 = \frac{V_A}{V_P} \quad (\text{Narrow-sense}), \qquad H^2 = \frac{V_G}{V_P} = \frac{V_A + V_D + V_I}{V_P} \quad (\text{Broad-sense}), \qquad R = h^2 S" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <strong>Breeder's Equation (Lush 1937):</strong> <Katex tex="R = \mu' - \mu" /> (Evolutionary response to selection) = <Katex tex="h^2" /> (Narrow-sense heritability) <Katex tex="\times S = \mu_s - \mu" /> (Selection differential).
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="algo-promo-card" style="margin-top: 1.25rem;">
