    <!-- Chapter 2 -->
    <section id="ch2">
      <h2>2. Linkage Disequilibrium (LD) & Chromosomal Architecture</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Linkage Disequilibrium (LD)</strong> is the non-random association of alleles at different loci across chromosomes in a population.
      </p>

      <h3>Mathematical Quantification of LD</h3>
      <p>
        Consider two biallelic loci with alleles <Katex tex="A/a" /> and <Katex tex="B/b" /> having marginal frequencies <Katex tex="p_A, p_a, p_B, p_b" /> and haplotype frequency <Katex tex="p_{AB}" />:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Coefficient of Linkage Disequilibrium (D)</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="D = p_{AB} - p_A p_B = p_{AB} p_{ab} - p_{Ab} p_{aB}" display={true} />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Lewontin's Normalized D' (1964) & Squared Correlation Coefficient r² (Hill & Robertson 1968)</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="D' = \frac{D}{D_{\max}}, \quad D_{\max} = \begin{cases} \min(p_A p_b, p_a p_B) & \text{if } D > 0 \\ \min(p_A p_B, p_a p_b) & \text{if } D < 0 \end{cases}, \qquad r^2 = \frac{D^2}{p_A (1 - p_A) p_B (1 - p_B)}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="|D'| = 1" /> indicates complete linkage (absence of at least one of the 4 gametes, meaning no historical recombination has separated the loci) &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="r^2 \in [0, 1]" /> measures statistical correlation, determining tag SNP imputation accuracy and GWAS statistical power.
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Recombination Mechanics & Exact Half-Life Decay of LD</h3>
      <p>
        Under meiotic recombination occurring at dimensionless rate <Katex tex="\theta \in [0, 0.5]" /> per generation, LD decays exponentially across generations:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Generational Decay & Exact Half-Life of Linkage Disequilibrium</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="D_t = D_0 (1 - \theta)^t \approx D_0 e^{-\theta t}, \qquad t_{1/2} = \frac{\ln(0.5)}{\ln(1 - \theta)} \approx \frac{0.693}{\theta} \quad (\theta \text{ as a fraction}) \approx \frac{69.3}{d} \quad (d \text{ in cM})" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <strong>P0 Exact Scaling:</strong> When recombination is given in centiMorgans (<Katex tex="d \text{ cM}" />, where <Katex tex="1 \text{ cM} \approx \theta = 0.01" />), the half-life is <Katex tex="t_{1/2} \approx 69.3 / d" /> generations.
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="deep-dive-callout callout-biology">
        <div class="deep-dive-callout-title">
          <Icon name="activity" size={15} /> Biological Drivers of Recombination: PRDM9 & Hotspots
        </div>
        <p>
          In humans and mice, meiotic recombination is non-uniform. Over 80% of crossovers are concentrated in narrow 1–2 kb <strong>recombination hotspots</strong> positioned by <strong>PRDM9</strong>, a zinc-finger histone methyltransferase that binds specific 13-mer sequence motifs (<Katex tex="\text{CCNCCNTNNCCNC}" />), depositing H3K4me3 and H3K36me3 to recruit SPO11 for programmed double-strand breaks (DSBs).
          <br/>
          This punctate recombination landscape creates discrete <strong>haplotype blocks</strong> (regions of high internal <Katex tex="r^2 > 0.8" /> spanning 10–100 kb) separated by recombination hotspots.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="algo-promo-card" style="margin-top: 1.25rem;">
