    <section id="ch10">
      <h2>10. The Modern Synthesis: Statistical Genetics Meets Deep Learning</h2>
      <p>
        Today, the frontier of statistical genetics is merging with regulatory sequence models (e.g. <strong>Enformer</strong>, <strong>Borzoi</strong>, <strong>AlphaGenome</strong>) and genomic language models (e.g. <strong>GPN-Star</strong>):
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Functionally Informed Fine-Mapping (PolyFun):</strong> Deep learning predictions across thousands of epigenetic tracks provide empirical priors (<Katex tex="\boldsymbol{\pi}" />) for SuSiE, prioritizing high-confidence causal candidates.</li>
        <li><strong>Deep Rare Variant Association Testing (DeepRVAT):</strong> Genomic functional embeddings weight rare missense and regulatory variants, boosting discovery power in large biobank cohorts.</li>
        <li><strong>Stratified LDSC with Learned Sequence Annotations:</strong> Annotating genomes with continuous foundation model embeddings quantifies tissue-specific partitioned heritability enrichments (<Katex tex="\tau^*" />).</li>
        <li><strong>Causal Validation Imperative:</strong> In silico mutagenesis (ISM) generates mechanistic hypotheses; high-throughput experimental perturbation (MPRA, CRISPR base editing, prime editing) provides the definitive gold standard.</li>
      </ul>

